---
<artifact id="tasks" schema="mohist-default">

<task>
Create the tasks artifact for the current change.
Implementation task graph for autonomous Mohist execution.
</task>

<dependencies>
Read these files for context before creating this artifact:

<dependency id="proposal" status="expected">
  <path>${{ openspecChangeDir }}/proposal.md</path>
  <description>Initial proposal document outlining the change</description>
</dependency>

<dependency id="specs" status="expected">
  <path>${{ openspecChangeDir }}/specs</path>
  <description>Detailed specifications for the change</description>
</dependency>

<dependency id="design" status="expected">
  <path>${{ openspecChangeDir }}/design.md</path>
  <description>Technical design document with implementation details</description>
</dependency>
</dependencies>

<output>
Write to: `${{ openspecChangeDir }}/tasks.json`
</output>

<instruction>
Create `tasks.json`, a machine-readable implementation plan for Mohist agents.

Tasks must be smallest independently testable units that deliver value. Split by outcome, not by files or layers.
Each task should be completable in one agent iteration and have clear acceptance criteria.

Reference specs for what needs to be built and design for how to build it.
</instruction>

<template>
<!-- Use this JSON structure for the output file. Fill in all fields. -->
{
  "project": "<project name>",
  "description": "<short change summary>",
  "tasks": [
    {
      "id": "T-001",
      "title": "Short task title",
      "spec": "specs/<capability>/spec.md#<requirement>",
      "description": "What to implement in 2-3 sentences",
      "acceptanceCriteria": ["verifiable criterion"],
      "priority": 1,
      "mode": "AFK",
      "type": "WRITE",
      "output": "expected output artifact or result",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "passes": false,
      "notes": ""
    }
  ]
}
</template>

<field_guidance>
- `id`: Task identifier (`T-001`, `T-002`, ...).
- `title`: Short task title.
- `spec`: Reference to a spec requirement when applicable.
- `description`: Outcome-focused implementation description.
- `acceptanceCriteria`: Verifiable criteria. For tasks with `spec`, these supplement spec scenarios.
- `priority`: Execution order. Lower number means earlier.
- `mode`: `AFK` or `HITL`. Use `AFK` unless human judgment is required.
- `type`: One of `WRITE`, `TEST`, `MIGRATE`, `CONFIG`, `REVIEW`.
- `output`: Expected output file, artifact, or observable result.
- `dependsOn`: Task IDs this task depends on. Dependencies must reference lower-priority tasks.
- `passes`: Always start as `false`.
- `notes`: Optional implementation notes.
</field_guidance>

<dependency_analysis>
Before writing final JSON:
1. Compare every pair of tasks.
2. Identify whether one task consumes output from another.
3. Add `dependsOn` whenever a later task needs a prior output.
4. Verify the dependency graph is a DAG.
5. Verify every `dependsOn` references an existing task with a strictly lower priority number.
</dependency_analysis>

<success_criteria>
- `tasks.json` is valid JSON.
- Every task is complete and deliverable.
- Every task has verifiable acceptance criteria.
- Dependencies are valid, acyclic, and point only to earlier tasks.
- Tasks map back to specs and design where applicable.
</success_criteria>

</artifact>
